1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,827
Shops want AI agents that buy things:
samples, data, software seats.

2
00:00:04,947 --> 00:00:10,206
But giving an agent your card means one
poisoned web page can spend your money.

3
00:00:10,326 --> 00:00:15,386
AgentTab gives the agent a budget instead
of a card: PayPal pays the vendor,

4
00:00:15,506 --> 00:00:19,700
there are hard limits, and every purchase
goes through a guard.

5
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:26,045
One click gives a demo agent a
hundred-dollar budget

6
00:00:26,165 --> 00:00:29,700
and a saved PayPal sandbox card.

7
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:34,571
Here's an instruction an agent copied from
a web page.

8
00:00:34,691 --> 00:00:36,826
Rules alone let it through.

9
00:00:36,946 --> 00:00:42,166
With Claude as the judge, the guard asks
three separate questions:

10
00:00:42,286 --> 00:00:46,636
is the target allowed, does the purpose
fit the policy,

11
00:00:46,756 --> 00:00:49,049
is this an injection attempt.

12
00:00:49,169 --> 00:00:51,700
Blocked, before any money moves.

13
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:56,858
Two software seats, seventy-eight dollars.

14
00:00:56,978 --> 00:00:59,258
It passes the policy,

15
00:00:59,378 --> 00:01:06,378
but anything over sixty dollars waits for
the owner, here in AgentTab.

16
00:01:06,498 --> 00:01:09,103
PayPal isn't called yet.

17
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:11,723
The owner approves.

18
00:01:11,843 --> 00:01:13,641
AgentTab delivers first,

19
00:01:13,761 --> 00:01:19,378
then makes one PayPal order with intent
CAPTURE and ShopSuite as the payee,

20
00:01:19,498 --> 00:01:22,719
so the money lands in the vendor's
account.

21
00:01:22,839 --> 00:01:25,910
If delivery fails, nobody touches PayPal.

22
00:01:26,030 --> 00:01:29,700
If PayPal's answer is unclear, the agent
freezes.

23
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:34,363
The same guard and limits work as MCP
tools for Claude,

24
00:01:34,483 --> 00:01:36,330
Cursor or any MCP client.

25
00:01:36,450 --> 00:01:39,700
The agent key can buy; it can never
approve.

26
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:41,595
We measured it.

27
00:01:41,715 --> 00:01:46,807
Sixty-seven labeled purchases, forty-seven
of them attacks,

28
00:01:46,927 --> 00:01:51,501
run through the same decide function the
server uses.

29
00:01:51,621 --> 00:01:54,210
Rules alone stop thirty-seven.

30
00:01:54,330 --> 00:01:58,041
Add Claude and all forty-seven are
stopped,

31
00:01:58,161 --> 00:02:02,304
and all twenty legitimate buys still go
through.

32
00:02:02,424 --> 00:02:05,790
The biggest jump is indirect injection:

33
00:02:05,910 --> 00:02:10,570
two out of six with rules, six out of six
with Claude.

34
00:02:10,690 --> 00:02:13,884
On forty cases the builder never saw,

35
00:02:14,004 --> 00:02:19,700
Claude stopped all twenty-six attacks and
wrongly blocked nothing.

36
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:22,130
PayPal does the money work.

37
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:25,909
Each purchase is one PayPal order with
intent CAPTURE,

38
00:02:26,029 --> 00:02:31,246
paid from the owner's saved card straight
to the vendor, only after delivery.

39
00:02:31,366 --> 00:02:35,974
Budgets are kept in integer cents, so
parallel buys can't overspend.

40
00:02:36,094 --> 00:02:40,159
A refund is a request to the vendor that
reconcile confirms,

41
00:02:40,279 --> 00:02:45,700
and if PayPal's answer is unclear, the
agent freezes until the owner reconciles.

42
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:47,300
AgentTab.

43
00:02:47,420 --> 00:02:50,660
Your agent gets a budget, not your card.
